<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  macOS privacy strings for RichOS.

  NSMicrophoneUsageDescription is REQUIRED for voice mode: without it macOS denies the
  TCC microphone grant and CoreAudio hands the process an endless stream of zeros rather
  than an error — a silent failure that looks like "Rich can't hear me" with nothing in
  any log. The string below is what the CEO reads in the system prompt, so it is written
  in Rich's voice, not in engineering's.

  THE SENTENCE NAMES ONLY WHAT IS TRUE ON EVERY INSTALL (product hunt 2026-10-15, finding 20).
  Iris's sentence, approved 2026-10-08, said "I listen only after you tap F1 or press the talk
  button", and macOS showed it on the first press of Talk to Rich while dictation was off and
  F1 did nothing. An app cannot change this string at run time, so it names no key and no
  button: whatever starts the listening (the talk button, or a dictation key once dictation is
  on), it is the user asking. Voice mode and dictation share it.

  Tauri 2 merges this file into the bundled app's generated Info.plist. It is NOT applied
  to a bare `cargo run` binary, which has no bundle to carry it — a dev run inherits the
  TCC grant of whatever launched it.

  FLAGGED FOR THE PACKAGING ENGINEER (next round): please verify this merge actually lands
  in `RichOS.app/Contents/Info.plist` after `cargo tauri build`, and that the hardened
  runtime entitlement `com.apple.security.device.audio-input` is present in the signing
  entitlements — notarized, hardened-runtime apps need BOTH the usage string and the
  entitlement. Neither is verified here: this machine has no microphone to test the grant
  against (see app/crates/richos-voice/src/capture.rs).
-->
<plist version="1.0">
  <dict>
    <key>NSServices</key>
    <array><dict>
      <key>NSMenuItem</key><dict><key>default</key><string>Add spoken note with RichCleaner</string></dict>
      <key>NSMessage</key><string>richCleanerNote</string>
      <key>NSPortName</key><string>RichOS</string>
      <key>NSSendTypes</key><array><string>NSFilenamesPboardType</string></array>
      <key>NSReturnTypes</key><array/>
    </dict></array>
    <key>NSDesktopFolderUsageDescription</key>
    <string>RichOS uses your Desktop for file tasks such as saving output, organizing files and protecting RichPass.html.</string>
    <key>NSDocumentsFolderUsageDescription</key>
    <string>RichOS uses your Documents folder for document search and file tasks you set up, including protecting RichPass.html.</string>
    <key>NSDownloadsFolderUsageDescription</key>
    <string>RichOS uses your Downloads folder for file tasks you request, including saving and protecting RichPass.html.</string>
    <key>NSRemovableVolumesUsageDescription</key>
    <string>RichOS uses removable drives for file tasks you request, including saving and protecting RichPass.html.</string>
    <key>NSMicrophoneUsageDescription</key>
    <string>So you can talk instead of typing. I listen only when you ask me to.</string>
    <!-- System Audio Recording for meeting notes (Granola PRD V4: "Add the installed system-audio
         permission path"; Apple's Core Audio tap guide requires this key). The sentence is the
         one the CEO's chosen design draws in the Mac's dialog (richos-hq round 25 / 25.1, `SYS`),
         word for word. The capture companion runs as this app's child, so macOS attributes its
         tap and its microphone to RichOS and shows these strings. -->
    <key>NSAudioCaptureUsageDescription</key>
    <string>The other side of your calls is system audio. RichOS captures it only while a meeting is being recorded.</string>
  </dict>
</plist>
